  "query_preview": "..\n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nThe persistent upper level ridge that has been the main driver of\nour weather the past two weeks or so will break down over the \nnext 24-48 hours as an upper level trough and a series of surface \ncold fronts push through the Treasure State. The first in this \nseries of fronts will begin to push through the area this \nafternoon, but ahead of that front temperatures will be rather \nwarm, with many lower elevation locales seeing temperatures rise \nthrough the upper 90s to around 105 in some of the warmest spots \n(pushing some areas into top 10 warmest day on record territory). \nAs the first front pushes through the area this afternoon and \nevening, winds will begin to increase first, then gradually shift \nfrom southwesterly to westerly as the front progresses. Winds will\nremain elevated through the overnight, keeping the atmosphere \nwell mixed overnight, which will keep us relatively warm with low \novernight humidity recovery. Temperatures will be noticeably \ncooler on Sunday behind this first front, but expect temperatures \nto still warm up to the 80s for most lower elevations. With winds \nremaining gusty and the humidity low, critical fire weather \nconditions will persist into the day on Sunday. It will not be \nuntil a second, stronger cold front pushes into the area Sunday \nevening that fire danger will begin to decrease, though the wind \nshift associated with this front will pose a risk to any fire \nsuppression operations. \n\nBehind this second front, Monday will be quite a bit cooler than \nwe have seen recently, with most population centers struggling to \nreach the mid 70s for afternoon highs. While these cooler \ntemperatures will likely feel nice to most folks, they will not \nlast long as upper level ridging begins to build back in across \nthe Western CONUS. While not as intense as the ridge we have had \nrecently, temperatures will once again warm up across the area, \nwith most lower elevation locales seeing low to mid 90s return for\nthe second half of the work week.\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nFire weather conditions today: There remains very high (>90%) \nconfidence in widespread critical fire weather conditions across \nNorth Central and Southwestern Montana today and Sunday as the \nseries of fronts mentioned above roll through the area. The first \nin this series of cold fronts will arrive later this evening, but \nsouthwesterly winds will increase across the area through the \nafternoon before gradually shifting to the west this evening. With\nwinds behind the first front remaining elevated through the \novernight, overnight temperatures will not drop as much as one \nmight expect, with lower elevations expected to generally get to \naround 60, though eastern Fergus County along the Missouri River \nBreaks may struggle to get below 70. With these warmer \ntemperatures, relative humidity values will struggle to rise \novernight, with some areas struggling to rise above 30% which will\nkeep fires a bit more active overnight. Winds and low humidity \nwill continue on Sunday, keeping our fire danger high ahead of \nanother, stronger cold front that will arrive late Sunday evening.\nWhile this front will eventually mean a reduction in fire weather\nconditions, the initial wind shift will be rather sudden and will\npose a danger to the men and women who are fighting the fires. \nLudwig",
